𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟰/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗦𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗰𝗲 𝗜𝘀 𝗗𝗼𝘄𝗻 𝗼𝗻 𝗟𝗶𝗻𝘂𝘅

🎯 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

“An application service is down on a production server. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I would not immediately restart the service.

First, I want to understand why the service stopped.

I would check the service status:

systemctl status <service-name>

Then I would check recent service logs:

journalctl -u <service-name> --since "30 minutes ago"

I would look for errors such as:

• Configuration issues
• Port conflicts
• Permission problems
• Missing files
• Dependency failures
• Out-of-memory issues
• Recent deployments or configuration changes

Next, I would verify whether the required port is listening:

ss -lntp

I would also check whether the server itself has resource issues:

uptime
free -m
df -h

If the service failed because of a configuration problem, I would fix the configuration according to the approved change procedure.

If the service is safe to restart and restarting is the approved recovery action:

systemctl restart <service-name>

Then I would immediately verify:

systemctl status <service-name>

and check the application endpoint or health check.

I would also monitor the logs to make sure the service remains stable.

Finally, I would document:

Issue → Investigation → Root Cause → Resolution → Preventive Action

For example:

Service down → Checked status → Found configuration error → Corrected configuration → Restarted service → Verified application → RCA

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗮𝘄𝗮𝘆

When a production service is down, don't just restart it and move on.

Understand:

𝗪𝗵𝘆 𝗱𝗶𝗱 𝗶𝘁 𝘀𝘁𝗼𝗽? → 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗳𝗮𝗶𝗹𝗲𝗱? → 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗱𝗼 𝗜 𝗿𝗲𝗰𝗼𝘃𝗲𝗿 𝗶𝘁? → 𝗛𝗼𝘄 𝗱𝗼 𝗜 𝗽𝗿𝗲𝘃𝗲𝗻𝘁 𝗶𝘁?

That's the troubleshooting mindset expected from a DevOps Engineer. 🚀
